catherine barbelsing is the new chair of  the social mobility commission in her  inaugural speech today she said that  britain is too focused on the rags to  rich's dream and people in the poorest  parts of society do not need to aim for  an oxbridge education catherine's also  known as britain's strictest  headmistress leading a school called the  michaela community school in a deprived  area of london her methods are often  seen to be controversial i spoke to her  a short time ago  catherine thanks for joining us on the  show today uh today you set out your  vision for britain and social mobility  or the government's new social  mobilities are but before we get on to  all of that um i wanted to talk to you  about education because for viewers that  don't know you you are branded now as  the country's strictest head teacher i  don't know if you love or loathe that or  just find it a bit  amusing but but let's start off about  talking about uh education there was a  documentary on itv uh about you and your  school  um and at the start of this documentary  one of your colleagues says it's not  like north korea  but those  unused to your beliefs about education  might think it did look a bit like north  korea so can you can you explain to the  viewers why it's not  because the place is just filled with  love you know the children are happy  that staff are happy  the staff love seeing the children  i see them for assembly every single day  in fact i do seven assemblies a week you  know because we've got seven year groups  um  there's just a real sense of purpose we  all know why we're there we're there  because we want to achieve and give the  children skills and knowledge that they  need to be able to make the most of  their lives and to enable social  mobility for our children because we're  in the inner city with children coming  from a variety of different backgrounds  and um we want to give them the best  chances that they can have in life  really and and just  you know if people that haven't seen the  documentary or know about your school i  mean it is really strict so there's no  talking in corridors i think there's a  line along the corridor yeah uh if you  drop your pencil you get a detention uh  well  not if you're not gonna if you were to  not bring your pen in you would get a  detention um you're dropping your pencil  that's fine that's fine but but i'm  trying to get across this it's really  really strict and it's unusually strict  compared perhaps with other uh state  schools why do you do it like that  yeah well the thing about strict is that  people tend to think that must mean that  you're mean but actually i would say  that it means you love the children  enough to keep your standards high for  them so the pen is a good example you  need to bring a pen into school  they're all taught to make sure that  they're packing their bags the night  before they've all got the same pencil  case with the same pens so there's real  consistency and those same pens are  expected for five years so they have two  black pens a blue pen a 2b pencil  they've got and they know exactly what  they need to be in there so  in that sense it's relatively  straightforward but then even if they do  forget it some of the children run a  little shop in the morning where they  sell pens for 10p so you can always pick  up a pen in the morning if you're if  you've forgotten it  and catherine what do you do with kids  that  can't do it or won't do it or do you  believe that it isn't that kids can't do  this  they can't can't adhere to your rules i  mean i mean  they all manage it uh the key thing is  to remember that um children have agency  and they can choose whether or not they  want to do something what do you do with  kids  what do you do with kids with special  education needs or kids with adhd kids  that might not be able  because of their own issues to focus in  the way that you require them to focus  in class do you have separate rules for  them or do those children have to  conform or do they not fit into the  school  well we have a normal intake of inner  city children so we have a variety of  children with different issues i would  say i mean there are a few children you  might need to slightly adapt for but  i would say that too often we allow  labels to force us to lower our  expectations of children um  and that they could achieve a lot more  if we just allowed ourselves to believe  in them despite the fact that they might  struggle so if they struggle in  something you might have to give them  some support in getting there to be able  to meet those standards some children  don't require much support and some  children require more support  um  but the key thing is to believe that  they can do it there has been a sense  from others in the teaching community  that you're kind of implicitly  criticizing uh other teachers or other  schools that do it differently to you  what do you say to that catherine  well if you're you can do things however  much you like as long as you're doing  right by the children i mean what i mean  is uh do you go to the school and visit  and think the children are well behaved  they're learning lots have they got  great gcse results are the children  happy are they loved if that's the case  then brilliant but if that's not the  case  then well we ought to be critical of  those schools shouldn't we i mean  shouldn't we as a society say that's not  good enough come on you've got to do  better now the thing is there are some  schools in genuine difficulty that are  trying very hard to get better and  that's brilliant but there are other  schools that are ideological in their  position that don't want to change  because  they don't believe  that children can do better what do you  mean by the ideological in their  position  well for instance they would find uh our  strict rules um  offensive they don't like that idea  but then if it's pointed out that but  look  there's lots of chaos here in this  school perhaps it would be better if you  had more rules they don't want to do  that and i would be critical of that you  know you spoke at the 2010 conservative  party conference and you became kind of  an emblem if you like of traditional  ways of teaching i mean some of your  fellow teachers make this point about  you when they're asked about you that  you're an excellent teacher clearly with  great ideas to offer  but you have become sometimes like a  weapon in a culture war in which what  you've just said about different methods  of teaching that other parents or  teachers might like somehow you're  critical of those and and that becomes a  culture war  what do you think about that do you do  you  disagree with that or do you not mind or  do you think it's okay  well i think it's  i think that we all need to have high  standards for all of our children and  there are children  right now  in classrooms with very poor behavior  who aren't able to learn and i'm the  chair of the social mobility commission  i believe very much in social mobility  for all of our children and if there are  children who aren't able to be upwardly  mobile because of the poor behavior in  their classrooms then that's something i  need to be critical about in what i've  read about you and what i've seen about  you in terms of your teaching you stress  the importance of adults modelling  standards and behavior for children  that's a really important value for you  do you think the prime minister is a  good role model for children  no i do not think so um  i don't uh  i wish he could be but he isn't um and  that is a bit sad but um  you know  i i can't get into the ins and outs  because i don't know enough about um  you know people say you know will she be  there should he not be there i don't  know you know uh i don't i i like boris  i don't think he's a bad guy you know um  but i don't  i don't know enough about what he's got  up to but i do not think that he's a  good role model for children what's not  good about him in what way  well his personal life for instance  uh  that that does make me sort of raise an  eyebrow um  his hair  how can i say his hair we've got strict  rules at school you know the other day i  saw a picture of him in the metro and i  looked at his hair and i thought oh my  goodness you know we expect our children  to have professional looking hair and i  looked at his hair and i thought gosh i  couldn't put him up on the wall and say  let's all have haircuts like him um now  you might think oh that's a bit pedantic  and that's a bit silly  but it isn't actually i think for our  children um  it's important to  uh look professional  and  sometimes boris looks professional but  sometimes he's not professional enough  for me let me put it that way okay so  family values because that's a small  secret family values exactly it's the  sort of thing that makes me pause a  little bit now and that's not to say  he just you know and actually i'd say  i'm i'm  relatively liberal about these things to  a certain extent you know but he does  kind of push the boat out a bit on that  own character  well and that's where you start and as i  said i don't know the ins and outs  enough about all the recent partygate  stuff you know i just don't know the  details because i'm too busy working to  follow all of that but  but it does sort of make you pause and  go  you know i certainly would never be able  to put him up and say be like him i  couldn't do that  look you just did a speech on social  mobility you talked about a fresh  approach i listened to it and at the  heart of it it seemed to me  that your approach is driven through a  combination of sort of spreading  opportunity more across the country and  an individual's own agency to kind of  change their lives rather than talking  about sort of structures holding people  back and structural inequalities is that  a fair interpretation of what your  approach will be in this job  yeah um  the thing is is that we want to look at  people who are backing the trends how is  it that in an unequal society that some  people are are upwardly mobile what do  they do to achieve that and um  so we want to look at them we also want  to look at uh  the ideas around what happens to the  many as opposed to the few often i think  as a society we are pushed um  to look at the people who start at the  bottom and then zoom to the top and it  makes a great hollywood film  it's romantic it's wonderful look i grew  up with nothing and now i'm this big ceo  and i eat expensive dinners but not all  of us necessarily want to do that and  some of us want to have shorter  mobilities and  it would be wrong for instance someone  comes from a home that's where lots of  unemployment and then they become a  manager at sainsbury's well isn't that  something to celebrate but often we  don't necessarily celebrate these the  these smaller achievements because we  only see the huge leap um as as the  success story and um  we would like to change the narrative  around that really going on to that idea  about the sort of success being defined  as i think you said a sort of  caretaker's daughter ending up in  oxbridge  and it actually being success defined as  someone whose parents are unemployed and  then have been long time unemployed and  then they get a job that that is their  version of social mobility that maybe  oxford is too much but becoming a  manager at sainsbury's is not right and  different people want to do different  things but of course we've got children  at school who are going to oxbridge and  it's really exciting and we're 100  behind that so you don't want to stop  doing that but not everyone can go to  oxbridge that's all but  one thing i did want to ask you though  because i think you went to oxford  didn't you yeah um because when it comes  to oxbridge the the higher education  minister said recently that those  universities shouldn't discriminate  against private school students going to  oxbridge that those organizers tilted  too much and they're too focused on  getting more state school kids in what's  your view of that  yeah i don't know to be honest i don't  know how much they're tilting one way or  the other it is important that um  the  the places that everyone wants to get  into whether it's the big law firms or  it's oxbridge and so on it's important  that they  recognize that they can get talent from  all different types of places and  they're not just coming from the private  sector or they're just not coming from  the state sector so they need to keep an  open mind and that's good but from my  point of view i'm thinking about how can  we make sure it's chair as the  commission how can we make sure as many  people as possible  are enabled to be able to make a success  of their lives finding a career that  they love which will give them purpose  and satisfaction because not all of us  want to be prime minister not always not  all of us want to be millionaires and  that's good i mean you're not prime  minister and you're very happy doing  your job you know  like that's the thing all of us want to  do different things i love being a  teacher i love being at school with the  children so  we all need to do what gives us it makes  us passionate you know what would you  like to do in the future i mean you've  you know you're extremely successful  professional in teaching you've now got  a public sector role advising the  government on social mobility is  politics something you might want to do  one day no never i mean in fact i feel  bad as chatting about boris because at  the end of the day he's just a man and  he's taken on a role that i would never  do and i would never do it because i  wouldn't want people sitting on a on a  television program chatting about me in  this way and yet  in that sense he has sacrificed you know  and given over his life to do that but  if you did do it you could implement  your school model across the entire  country no  no that's the thing you need to win  people's hearts and minds you can't do  that in fact in many ways you're very  powerless as prime minister um  i think we misunderstand just how much  power our prime minister actually has  catherine thank you so much that was  fascinating thanks for coming on thank  you

